# Quarto reveal.js slides (Slidecrafting)

Whenever the user asks to create, restyle, animate, or present
**Quarto reveal.js** slides (`format: revealjs` in a `.qmd`, or work
under `slides/` / similar deck folders), follow this workflow.

## 1. Ensure the global slidecrafting skills are installed

Expected personal Cursor skills (from
[EmilHvitfeldt/slidecrafting-book.com](https://github.com/EmilHvitfeldt/slidecrafting-book.com)):

- `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs/SKILL.md` (main router)
- `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs-fragment/SKILL.md` (optional; custom fragments)
- `~/.cursor/skills/auto-animate-elements/SKILL.md` (optional; auto-animate casts)

Also acceptable mirrors: `~/.agents/skills/<same-names>/`.

**If `quarto-revealjs` is missing**, stop designing slides from scratch
and **suggest installing** the global skill first, e.g.:

```bash
npx skills add emilhvitfeldt/slidecrafting.com --skill quarto-revealjs
# or all three:
npx skills add emilhvitfeldt/slidecrafting.com
```

(Requires Node ≥ 22.20.) Then continue after the skill is present.

## 2. Always use the skill for reveal.js decks

1. **Read** `~/.cursor/skills/quarto-revealjs/SKILL.md` (or the
   `~/.agents/skills/` copy) **before** writing or heavily editing a
   reveal.js deck.
2. Open the matching `references/*.md` file(s) the skill points to
   (theme, layout, code/plots, fragments, auto-animate, presenting).
3. Prefer Slidecrafting conventions over generic Quarto/reveal.js
   improvisation: theme SCSS, columns/absolute layout, fragment and
   auto-animate patterns, speaker notes / PDF export as documented
   there and at <https://slidecrafting-book.com>.
4. Cross-check features against official Quarto reveal.js docs
   (<https://quarto.org/docs/presentations/revealjs/>) **and** the
   Slidecrafting book (<https://slidecrafting-book.com/>) when choosing
   YAML options, extensions, layout, or animation patterns. Prefer
   documented Quarto / Slidecrafting features over ad-hoc HTML/CSS.
5. For custom fragment CSS/JS or multi-slide auto-animate element
   casts, also read `quarto-revealjs-fragment` and/or
   `auto-animate-elements` when those skills are installed.

Do **not** invent ad-hoc slide CSS/layout systems when the skill
already covers the pattern.

### Converting legacy decks (PDF / PPTX / Markdown)

Source materials often exist as PDF, PowerPoint, and Markdown of the
same talk. Prefer **Markdown** for Quarto authoring when the text is
already structured; use **PDF** when you need visual fidelity or when
Markdown was a lossy export; use **PPTX** mainly to recover embedded
figures. They are complementary, not interchangeable.

To turn a PDF into Markdown, use Stephen Turner’s **pdfdown** web API:
<https://stephenturner.github.io/pdfdown/> (then clean headings and
rebuild as Quarto reveal.js with Slidecrafting patterns).


## 3. Slide design lessons

Adapted from
[VibePresenting — Slide Design Lessons](https://github.com/jiadongm/VibePresenting/blob/main/SKILL.md#slide-design-lessons)
for Quarto reveal.js. Where these conflict with Slidecrafting
(`quarto-revealjs` and its `references/`), **Slidecrafting wins** for
theme, layout primitives, fragments, and auto-animate.

### Content principles

- One headline claim per slide. Prefer bullet points over full sentences.
- Prefer diagrams and simple tables over dense equations.
- Use equations only after the intuition is clear. Add dimensions to
  matrix equations when introducing factorisation.
- Keep language concrete before technical notation. Introduce every new
  concept with one concrete example before any notation.
- When using paper figures, preserve aspect ratio and crop/resize for
  the message of the slide.

### Overflow is the primary failure mode

Reveal.js slides have a fixed viewport. Content that exceeds it
overflows with little or no render-time warning.

- **Always render after edits** (`quarto render …`) and visually check
  the changed slide. Do not assume content will fit.
- When combining a diagram with content below it, budget vertical space
  explicitly. Tighten margins, padding, and font sizes on the upper
  element to leave room.
- If a slide overflows, cut content or tighten spacing — do not shrink
  fonts below readability.

### Font-size hierarchy

For information-dense slides, keep a consistent hierarchy:

| Element | Size |
|---|---|
| Diagram node labels | 18–20px |
| Card / section headers | 14–16px |
| Supporting detail, bullet text | 12–13px |
| Affiliations, metadata, attributions | 10–11px |

Going below 10px is unreadable from the back of a seminar room. If you
need smaller text to fit, the slide has too much content.

### Styles: Slidecrafting first, then reuse rules

- Prefer Slidecrafting / Quarto reveal.js layout and theme patterns
  (columns, absolute positioning, SCSS theme variables) over inventing
  new systems.
- Use the deck’s existing CSS classes first.
- For **one-off** adjustments on a single slide, inline `style` is fine;
  do not add a new CSS class for something used once.
- Add a new CSS class only when a style is reused across three or more
  slides. Do not churn global SCSS/CSS for one slide.

### Collaborator / comparison grids

For collaborator panels, comparison cards, and multi-item rows, CSS
grid is acceptable when Slidecrafting layout primitives are a poor fit:

```html
<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(N, 1fr); gap:12px; text-align:center;">
  <div>
    <img src="figs/people/Name.jpg"
         style="height:72px; width:72px; object-fit:cover; border-radius:50%;">
    <div style="font-size:14px; margin-top:4px;">Name</div>
    <div style="font-size:11px; color:#5f6b7a;">Affiliation</div>
    <div style="font-size:11px; color:#5f6b7a;">Role</div>
  </div>
</div>
```

For missing headshots, use a grey circle with initials:

```html
<div style="height:72px; width:72px; border-radius:50%; background:#d0d5dd;
            display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;
            margin:0 auto; font-size:24px; color:#6b7280;">AB</div>
```

### Backbone diagrams as navigation

When a talk has a unifying programme diagram, show it at the opening,
re-show a compact version at major section transitions, and close with
it.

- On first appearance, build in stages if order matters (fragments /
  auto-animate via Slidecrafting). On re-appearances, show it static.
- Colour-code methods in the diagram and reuse those colours when
  referencing the same methods later.

### Figures and attribution

- Check whether requested assets already exist locally before creating
  new ones.
- Use local relative paths from the slide source directory.
- Keep figure attributions short and visually quiet:

```html
<div style="font-size:8px; color:#5f6b7a; text-align:right;">
Author et al., Journal, Year
</div>
```

### Editing guardrails

- Do not replace a figure with a placeholder unless the user asks.
- Do not change slide count, section structure, or title/abstract
  without explicit instruction.
- Do not silently remove citations or attributions.
- Keep unrelated existing content intact.
- Preserve the deck’s British English spelling, citation style, and
  colour palette.
- Treat user comments and recent manual edits as primary specifications;
  do not rewrite locked sections unless the user reopens them.
- Hide executed code on slides unless the user wants it shown
  (`echo: false` / Quarto chunk options).
- When in doubt, simplify the slide source rather than adding
  abstraction.

